    "long_instructions": "# Usability Checklist\r\n\r\n### Do This:\r\n\r\n1. Complete the task below from your selected user’s perspective.  \r\n2. Then return to your Activity Guide and complete **Part 2: Usability Checklist** based on your experience.\r\n\r\n### Task\r\n\r\n1. Find one **dog** that is:\r\n\t- Under 2 years old  \r\n\t- Good with kids  \r\n\t- Small  \r\n2. Open **Details** to confirm you've found an appropriate dog.  \r\n3. Click **Adopt**.",
    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "ask"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
      }
    },
    "teacher_markdown": "## Teaching Tips (Usability Checklist)\r\n\r\n- **Require students to stay in character.**  \r\nStudents must complete the task from their assigned user’s perspective, not their own. Listen for profile-based reasoning rather than personal preference.\r\n\r\n- **Push for evidence from the interface.**\r\nWhen students complete the checklist, require them to reference specific parts of the app (text size, layout, button placement, density of information).\r\n\r\n- **Probe surface-level answers.**\r\nIf a student says “It was confusing,” ask: “Confusing for who?” and “What specifically caused that?” Push them to tie each response back to a user need.\r\n\r\n- **Watch for task breakdown points.**\r\nNotice where students hesitate: scanning the cards, locating age, confirming size, opening Details, or clicking Adopt. These friction points often reveal usability gaps.\r\n\r\n- **Reinforce that usability depends on context.**\r\nIf one student says the app was fine and another says it was difficult, highlight that both can be correct depending on the user profile.\r\n\r\n- **Encourage specific improvement suggestions.**\r\nWhen students propose changes, require them to complete the sentence: “This would help _ because _.”"
  },
